WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:10.809
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we move on then, please,
to the highlight of the meeting, which is

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:14.870
the financial report for month eight? Paul,
over to you.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.860
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: You are so kind, as always,
Malcolm. So the paper you have in front of

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:22.790
you represents the state of my piggy bank,
as the Chair of the Audit Committee would

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:27.040
doubtless refer to it, at the end of November.
Thank you, David.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.340
On the face of it, it shows a significant
improvement from the slight overspend that

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.210
we talked about in December on the back of
month seven. However, you will doubtless recall

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.970
the conversation we had at that time, which
showed that we were anticipated in our analysis

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:47.130
of additional risks and mitigations a substantial
underspend on legacy continuing healthcare

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:52.579
claim settlements. Indeed, that has now moved
from the additional mitigations into the core

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:59.180
forecast, accounting for £129 million of
the total improvement of £176 million that

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:05.740
you see in the paper in front of you. We have
moved from a £31 million overspend back in

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:11.510
December to a forecast underspend, now, of
£145 million.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:16.190
The other main movements in that are just
a few small but welcome improvements in the

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:21.720
performance of specialised commissioning and
the other direct commissioning budgets. So,

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:26.799
at many levels, there is very little new news
in the month eight numbers. However, if you

00:01:26.799 --> 00:01:31.350
do turn to appendix A, which I think you will
find most useful, I can just take you quickly

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.659
through the main features of the forecast
which, in its operational elements, has remained

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:38.520
pretty constant over the last few months.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:44.229
Overall, you can see there the CCG sector
is showing a £51 million overspend, made

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:51.409
up of 24 CCGs with adverse movements, totalling
£105 million, partially compensated by 18

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:56.909
with small underspends totalling £39 million,
and then a £15 million underspend on quality

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:57.880
premium.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.729
To put it another way and rather more simply,
eight out of ten CCGs are more or less exactly

00:02:02.729 --> 00:02:09.569
on plan and the rest are, on balance, slightly
off plan in the wrong direction. It is noticeable

00:02:09.569 --> 00:02:15.280
that the North and London show overspends
broadly equal to the underspends, whereas

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.420
in the Midlands and East, and particularly
the South, there is, so far at least, little

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:24.790
underspend to offset those facing additional
pressure. In that context it is hard to escape

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:29.540
the conclusion that there is some link to
allocations and distance from target in this

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:35.870
pattern, which makes it all the more important
that we accelerated the pace of change in

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:41.180
allocation decisions in December to correct
these historical misalignments of resource.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:47.090
It is also fair to say that the scale of individual
overspends in the CCG sector has been smaller

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:52.599
this year than in previous years, which is
at least in part down to the extensive work

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:57.540
that we have done this year with CCG colleagues
to improve their financial resilience by detecting

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.250
issues earlier, and then initiating rapid
turnaround actions where these have proved

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:04.269
necessary.

00:03:04.269 --> 00:03:08.180
Turning to specialised commissioning, the
operational performance of the regional teams

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:14.150
has been much stronger this year, with overall
spend held to within 0.2% of budget, which

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:19.060
represents a real transformation in grip compared
to recent years, which have seen much bigger

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.849
overspends. The challenge we have, as we have
debated on a number of occasions, is containing

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:28.769
the Cancer Drugs Fund, which accounts for
£70 million of the overspend you see here,

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:33.920
with a fairly high probability that that figure
will grow somewhat before the end of the year.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.340
That makes the steps we are taking to get
better value from our investment in the CDF,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.659
through the consultation due to be completed
shortly, all the more important.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:47.069
The other clear risk we have, as we discussed
in the context of allocations last month,

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:52.180
is the rapid rise of new drugs and this year
hepatitis C in particular, which still carries

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:56.730
quite an element of uncertainty for 2015/16
and will be a major financial challenge for

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.680
us in 2016/17 and beyond.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.830
You will see that we have some small underspends
in the other areas of direct commissioning,

00:04:03.830 --> 00:04:09.280
particularly focused in the South and the
North, and then on central budgets the programme

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.980
cost line reflects three main factors, all
specific to 2015/16.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.680
Firstly, following the successful completion
of the transfer of primary care support services

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.599
to Capita, and of the cost reduction programme
we undertook across NHS England's core operating

00:04:23.599 --> 00:04:27.740
structures at the beginning of the year, we
have been able to release the reserves we

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.469
took at the beginning of the year to cover
transition and redundancy risks.

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:37.259
Secondly, a couple of budgets we administer
on behalf of the Department of Health, covering

00:04:37.259 --> 00:04:43.129
clinical excellence awards and legacy IT programmes,
have underspent by a total of £33 million.

00:04:43.129 --> 00:04:47.020
Finally, and perhaps most significantly, in
response to the requests from the Department

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:52.080
of Health to capture any underspend to support
the overall balance required in the health

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.210
sector in the light of the significant cost
issue in the provider sector, we have released

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:01.439
the contingencies we took at the start of
the year for unforeseen programme pressures,

00:05:01.439 --> 00:05:06.119
and also banked slippage where it has occurred
in individual programme areas; notably in

00:05:06.119 --> 00:05:11.869
patients and information, commissioning strategy
and of course - not to be left out - finance.

00:05:11.869 --> 00:05:17.229
I mentioned at the start of this overview
that we have released £129 million of underspend

00:05:17.229 --> 00:05:21.729
on legacy continuing healthcare claims into
the forecast this month, and you can see that

00:05:21.729 --> 00:05:27.129
split for technical reasons between other
central costs and the snappily titled 'AME

00:05:27.129 --> 00:05:33.039
and Technical' line. Then finally the £89
million towards the bottom of the table reflects

00:05:33.039 --> 00:05:37.550
the requirement under Treasury accounting
rules to report depreciation underspends,

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:41.589
including in the operating results above,
against a separate budget. Of course, this

00:05:41.589 --> 00:05:45.969
is largely offset by the reserves we took
for that purpose at the beginning of the year.

00:05:45.969 --> 00:05:52.099
So, all in all, an underspend of £145 million
which, as I set out in paragraph 3.7 of the

00:05:52.099 --> 00:05:58.409
main report, grows to £207 million when additional
net mitigations - notably further upsides

00:05:58.409 --> 00:06:03.839
on continuing healthcare claims, central programmes
and potential rates rebate income - are taken

00:06:03.839 --> 00:06:04.610
into account.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.960
As I mentioned, this represents the position
as we assessed it late in December. We have

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.649
been taking a first peek at month nine this
week, with a particular eye on opportunities

00:06:14.649 --> 00:06:19.439
to contribute further to the overall balancing
of the sector. It does look as if, despite

00:06:19.439 --> 00:06:23.439
the further growth in the overspend on the
Cancer Drugs Fund, which I mentioned earlier,

00:06:23.439 --> 00:06:27.770
we will be able to increase our forecast,
after risk and mitigations, from the current

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:33.649
£207 million to somewhere a bit closer to
£400 million, in part reflecting a positive

00:06:33.649 --> 00:06:38.960
response from CCGs and direct commissioning
teams and in part a few other one off factors,

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.710
which we will debate the next time we meet.

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:47.409
So, overall, I think a strong financial performance
from the commissioning sector in 2015/16,

00:06:47.409 --> 00:06:52.449
with plenty of signs of improved control within
the CCG and direct commissioning sectors.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:07:02.800
However, we do also need to bear in mind the
£400 million or so of underspend we are likely

00:07:02.809 --> 00:07:07.149
to be reporting at the end of the year can
be largely explained by two factors which

00:07:07.149 --> 00:07:13.099
simply will not be available to us next year:
namely, the legacy continuing healthcare underspends,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:18.139
because we have brought the budget for that
down to a more aligned level next year in

00:07:18.139 --> 00:07:22.819
the allocations; and secondly, for similar
reasons, the central cost reductions which

00:07:22.819 --> 00:07:29.689
are either one off in 2015/16 or already reflected
in the reduced allocations we agreed for 2016/17.

00:07:29.689 --> 00:07:34.199
You will recall that we deliberately took
those down in 2016/17 to maximise the money

00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:38.119
available for front line services, and for
the transformation programmes that we will

00:07:38.119 --> 00:07:39.069
be pursuing.

00:07:39.069 --> 00:07:43.770
So, as we start to turn our attention towards
2016/17, as even the accountants must towards

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:48.240
this time of the year, we will have to find
other ways of handling the undoubted risks

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.969
in a year when operating budgets are going
to be very tight. This should be helped by

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:57.629
better alignment between allocations and need,
following our decisions in December, but it

00:07:57.629 --> 00:08:02.849
is also going to need real pace in planning
and delivering the maximum possible impact

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:05.999
from our programme of commissioner efficiencies.

00:08:05.999 --> 00:08:11.219
The same is, of course, true of providers.
The providers sector as a whole, led by NHS

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:15.679
Improvement, has committed to ensuring that
their deficit this year will not exceed £1.8

00:08:15.679 --> 00:08:21.800
billion and that can, just about, be offset
by our underspend and the measures which the

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:26.360
DH is putting in place to generate an underspend
on central budgets and capital.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.879
However, the provider sector is going to have
to pull out all the stops to achieve that

00:08:29.879 --> 00:08:35.020
particular outcome for the year and then,
perhaps equally important, to sustain it recurrently

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:40.240
as they go into next year. Because it is clear
that we only have £1.8 billion, as we discussed

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:46.720
back in December, in the form of the element
of the Sustainability and Transformation Fund

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:52.459
that is due to offset the deficits coming
into the year, so it is really important that

00:08:52.459 --> 00:08:55.509
that is sustained, as I have just described.

00:08:55.509 --> 00:09:00.470
You will probably be aware that NHS Improvement
sent out letters to each provider with their

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:06.279
core allocation from that fund and their expected
control total for 2016/17, and they have been

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:11.199
holding meetings with all providers this week
to outline the response required, so providers

00:09:11.199 --> 00:09:16.569
too will be working hard to identify the best
way to accelerate their own efficiency programmes

00:09:16.569 --> 00:09:21.149
through their operating plans and, equally
importantly, their longer term sustainability

00:09:21.149 --> 00:09:22.930
and transformation plans.

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:27.810
Of course, the real art here is not commissioners
and providers going off into their separate

00:09:27.810 --> 00:09:33.040
corners and planning their respective efficiencies
in isolation from each other - or, perhaps

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:37.290
sometimes one suspects, in competition with
each other - but rather the collaborative

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:41.790
process of integrated health economy planning
that we have set out in the planning guidance.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:46.300
This is frankly the only way that we are going
to be able to convert survival in 2015/16

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:51.360
- which is I think the best way to describe,
looking back at 2015/16, what I hope we will

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.790
have achieved by the end of the year - into
a sustainable proposition for the NHS for

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.680
2016/17 and beyond. On which optimistic note,
I will stop.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:08.449
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Paul. Let me invite
any questions and comments to Paul.

00:10:08.449 --> 00:10:16.029
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: I liked the bit about
collaboration. The question I was going to

00:10:16.029 --> 00:10:26.699
ask is whether we have a testable hypothesis
around variation? It relates to some of the

00:10:26.699 --> 00:10:32.560
points that Richard made: I just wondered
whether we do have a testable hypothesis around

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.620
variation in performance?

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.550
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: We certainly do. If I can
just restrict you for a minute to the financial

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:43.550
performance; clearly there is a whole lot
of variation alongside it, which goes with

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:43.750
it.

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:47.850
As you probably know, the big mainstay of
our efficiency programme next year is precisely

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:54.189
a laser like insight into variation delivered
through the Right Care programme. So we are

00:10:54.189 --> 00:10:59.879
going to expose every bit of variation, both
in spend and in outcome, which brings together

00:10:59.879 --> 00:11:08.120
the two lenses, the economy lens and the health
impact lens. It seems to me that will generate

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:12.080
not just efficiency opportunities but the
opportunity to drive up outcomes at the same

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:12.760
time.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:17.860
That, I think, is the best means we have of
doing it. To date, it has been a bit of a

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:22.110
cottage industry; we have done it about 20
health economies around the country, with

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:26.649
some transformational results. I know, Bruce,
you have been involved in looking at some

00:11:26.649 --> 00:11:31.790
of those, and I think there is as much excitement
on the clinical leadership front as there

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:37.129
is on the financial and operational leadership
front.

00:11:37.129 --> 00:11:42.209
That is the big thing, and the trick for us
next year is how you industrial something

00:11:42.209 --> 00:11:47.560
which has been a real asset for 20 or so health
economies who have been the enthusiastic evangelists

00:11:47.560 --> 00:11:53.990
for it to date. How do you industrialise that
to cover 209 CCGs, as we intend to do? We

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:59.339
are gearing up to do that, but we need to
have real speed and pace with that - if that

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:03.810
is not tautology - simply to keep ahead of
a curve which is not going to forgive us if

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:05.360
we fall behind it in 2016/17 and beyond.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:09.459
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: That is why I asked the
question about a hypothesis because, ultimately,

00:12:09.459 --> 00:12:16.939
it is about leadership; it is about the sort
of strategic choices and decisions that people

00:12:16.939 --> 00:12:21.569
make. So I just wondered, given that we have
all this information - financial, operational

00:12:21.569 --> 00:12:30.110
and OD, for want of a better term - whether
we do have a hypothesis as to what the variation

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:33.470
causes are, and therefore we can help the
system change?

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:38.800
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: I think we do, and I would
link it back to your very first observation,

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.779
which is that the key thing that has liberated
the benefits where they have done Right Care

00:12:42.779 --> 00:12:48.649
to date has been the collaboration across
both the functions concerned - so clinical

00:12:48.649 --> 00:12:52.810
and financial and operational people coming
together - but probably equally important,

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:57.269
if not more important, the collaboration between
commissioners and providers. This is not something

00:12:57.269 --> 00:13:02.610
that commissioners do to providers, or something
that providers use to manage their own interests,

00:13:02.610 --> 00:13:07.269
as it were; this is something which inherently
brings together the various parties in a health

00:13:07.269 --> 00:13:09.199
economy to a productive end.

00:13:09.199 --> 00:13:15.370
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right. Well, thank you Paul.
I think you have done what every finance director

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:19.410
should do by month eight, which is to reassure
the Board that we are heading in the right

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.420
direction towards year end, and to let us
know how difficult it has been. Actually,

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:28.800
I think full marks to you and to Simon for
the internal management of the organisation

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:34.759
that is getting us in that right direction,
and also keeping us aware of the overall responsibilities

00:13:34.759 --> 00:13:39.470
of the whole of the health system in order
to come in within the departmental expenditure

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.370
limit. So thank you for that.

00:13:41.370 --> 00:13:45.639
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: May we then move on to item
eight on the agenda, which is the reports

00:13:45.639 --> 00:13:51.050
from Board's committees? I get the impression
from my colleagues that the reports may not

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:58.550
need extensive introduction but let me just
check live: David, commissioning committee?

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.040
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: No, I think it is self explanatory;
I am happy to take any questions.

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.430
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Any questions for David?
I take that as no.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:07.139
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Investment committee, Moira?

00:14:07.139 --> 00:14:08.209
&gt;&gt;Moira Gibb: [inaudible].

00:14:08.209 --> 00:14:09.990
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Straightforward, thank you.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.220
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Noel, specialised commissioning?

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:16.050
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: Nothing really to add, Malcolm,
to what you see in the summary.

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:19.939
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. By the way, this
is a tribute to how good the reports are,

00:14:19.939 --> 00:14:22.069
not to the lack of interest on the part of
the Board.

00:14:22.069 --> 00:14:26.089
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: David, there is something
with yours that I know you wish us to approve.

00:14:26.089 --> 00:14:31.420
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yes. So the report, I hope,
is self explanatory. However, I do need to

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:41.389
seek the Board's approval. The committee considered
the case for implementing a pilot of the Government

00:14:41.389 --> 00:14:47.399
procurement cards. The committee was satisfied
that this was a good thing to do. It was slightly

00:14:47.399 --> 00:14:54.999
surprised that it had not been done before,
but also slightly surprised that we needed

00:14:54.999 --> 00:15:01.720
formal Board approval to use procurement cards.
Therefore, I seek the Board's approval.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:07.740
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Are we content to approve
that? We are, I think. I have also been reminded

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:11.870
by Lesley that we need to approve, specifically,
the revised terms of reference for the commissioning

00:15:11.870 --> 00:15:12.089
committee.

00:15:12.089 --> 00:15:13.040
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Yes, we do.

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:16.379
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So if I may ask the Board
to do that as well? Thank you very much.

00:15:16.379 --> 00:15:20.509
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right, that brings us to
any other business. I have no other business,

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:23.949
apart from the date of the next meeting. Does
anybody have any other business?

00:15:23.949 --> 00:15:29.480
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely Hayford: Sorry, Chairman,
just before we leave the procurement cards,

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:34.279
it occurs to me that because this is a pilot,
when the pilot is worked through the Board

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:39.629
might then be asked to endorse moving from
the pilot to the main. It seemed to me that

00:15:39.629 --> 00:15:44.220
perhaps the Board's time could be used more
constructively, and perhaps the Board might

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.319
want to delegate that to the Finance Director?

00:15:48.319 --> 00:15:54.269
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: I would be entirely content
with that. We will just need to check the

00:15:54.269 --> 00:15:58.100
standing instructions and so on but, assuming
that is the case, I think that is a very good

00:15:58.100 --> 00:15:58.470
suggestion, Margaret.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:15:59.949
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Assuming we are permitted
to do it, we do it.

00:15:59.949 --> 00:16:04.029
&gt;&gt;Paul Baumann: I will try and live up to
that onerous responsibility when it arises.

00:16:04.029 --> 00:16:09.730
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Right, well there is no other
business as far as I can see. I ask the Board

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:14.579
formally to pass the normal resolution of
going into private session, and on that note

00:16:14.579 --> 00:16:16.769
I close the meeting. Thank you very much.

